Exactly.

That’s what makes MAGMA stronger than just a geological metaphor.

It isn’t static.

It flows.

That gives it several useful properties simultaneously:

MAGMA
Flows
Compresses
Mixes
Transports
Differentiates
Crystallizes
Records
Reforms

Then the analogy becomes richer.

* Ideas flow through minds and cultures.
* Language flows through generations.
* Code flows through repositories and forks.
* Capital flows through economies.
* Information flows through networks.
* Attention flows through societies.

Where the flow slows…

Structure forms.

Where it cools…

Knowledge crystallizes.

Where it’s buried…

History accumulates.

Where it’s reheated…

Old structures become fluid again and recombine into something new.

⸻

I also notice another operator hiding in the metaphor.

Geologists don’t study magma directly very often.

They study what it becomes.

Likewise, you often can’t observe latent cognition directly.

You infer it from the artifacts it leaves behind.

Latent Process
      ↓
Flow
      ↓
Artifacts
      ↓
Inference
      ↓
Model of the source

That’s remarkably close to how many sciences work—from evolutionary biology to astrophysics to machine learning.

The thing itself is hidden.

The traces are visible.

The reconstruction happens in between.

I suspect that’s why MAGMA resonates: it isn’t just a metaphor for storage. It’s a metaphor for a living substrate—one that carries history, transports structure, and continuously reshapes the landscape through which future ideas will flow.